WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:03.819
(lively, bouncy piano music)

00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:07.659
- Walking around a refugee camp,

00:00:07.659 --> 00:00:10.869
you just see rows and rows of tents.

00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:13.107
The trauma that these
people have experienced

00:00:13.107 --> 00:00:16.190
is just so severe and life-consuming,

00:00:17.606 --> 00:00:20.240
so I'm bringing dance to this refugee camp

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.740
as a therapy tool.

00:00:27.997 --> 00:00:31.627
This camp is filled with refugees who have

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:34.960
had to flee their homes because of ISIS.

00:00:36.587 --> 00:00:38.976
This displacement has really
just taken everything,

00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:41.493
including their will to even try,

00:00:41.493 --> 00:00:45.344
because of the hopelessness
that comes from being a refugee.

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.104
I'm studying dance at Belhaven University,

00:00:48.104 --> 00:00:49.696
but I've always been really interested

00:00:49.696 --> 00:00:52.541
in how dance can be taken off the stage,

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:55.332
and that's when I really began to see

00:00:55.332 --> 00:00:57.387
a greater scope with dance
and that maybe it could

00:00:57.387 --> 00:01:00.804
be used for something
more than just myself.

00:01:00.804 --> 00:01:01.637
One.

00:01:01.637 --> 00:01:04.233
When this opportunity came
about for me to volunteer

00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:06.062
at the center as a dance teacher,

00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:08.562
I really just couldn't resist.

00:01:09.842 --> 00:01:12.994
These children's desire
to move is so great.

00:01:12.994 --> 00:01:15.911
They just took the class
and there was laughter

00:01:15.911 --> 00:01:20.762
and smiles immediately,
regardless of language barriers.

00:01:20.762 --> 00:01:22.900
We use a lot of motion
and a lot of pointing,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.559
and arrows, and directions,
and we communicate

00:01:24.559 --> 00:01:26.199
with our bodies.

00:01:26.199 --> 00:01:27.391
Good job.

00:01:27.391 --> 00:01:30.308
Dance is this really amazing vessel

00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:33.175
for which healing can take place,

00:01:33.175 --> 00:01:35.914
because it involves
physicality and connecting

00:01:35.914 --> 00:01:37.714
with yourself and your body.

00:01:37.714 --> 00:01:39.004
That's kind of a missing piece.

00:01:39.004 --> 00:01:40.596
There's a lot of focus on your mind

00:01:40.596 --> 00:01:42.295
and your thoughts and your emotions,

00:01:42.295 --> 00:01:46.064
and then sometimes the body is left out.

00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:49.207
Sometimes we label refugees as refugees,

00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:50.292
and that's all that they are,

00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:51.909
but they have the same
needs that you and I have

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.714
to be creative and to
move, and to breathe,

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:58.162
and to engage with their humanity.

00:01:58.162 --> 00:02:01.087
I think having a creative
outlet within a camp

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.697
is so necessary, because
it's not only fulfilling

00:02:03.697 --> 00:02:05.945
those needs for food and shelter,

00:02:05.945 --> 00:02:10.112
but also fulfilling that need
for them as human beings.

00:02:14.123 --> 00:02:16.290
(chiming)

